FreeLinX/ports - base/doom : patch/port notes
=============================================

This port vendors no source changes into the upstream doomgeneric source it
builds from (dist/doomgeneric-master.tar.gz).  Everything about the FreeLinX
build is expressed in the port `Makefile` (static link ceremony, toolchain
flags) and one FreeLinX-owned file:

  backends/doomgeneric_ppm.c
      A FreeLinX addition (not upstream).  An alternative `doomgeneric_p`-
      style backend that writes rendered frames to PPM files instead of the
      Linux framebuffer, so the Doom render/game core can be verified on a
      machine / kernel with no /dev/fb0.  It is compiled into the `doombench`
      verification binary, never into the rootfs `doom` binary.

The delivered rootfs binary `doom` uses the upstream `linuxvt` backend
(doomgeneric_linuxvt.c), which writes each frame to /dev/fb0 via the standard
FBIO mmap interface and reads input from evdev.  It needs:

  - a Linux framebuffer device /dev/fb0 at runtime
  - /dev/input/event* (keyboard) for input

Runtime data: DOOM requires a .wad data file (e.g. the freely redistributable
shareware doom1.wad) alongside the binary.  The .wad is game data belonging to
id Software and is intentionally NOT vendored into FreeLinX.

Known kernel blocker (see base/doom): the current FreeLinX 6.6.21 kernel
config enables CONFIG_VT/CONFIG_VGA_CONSOLE but disables the framebuffer
subsystem (no CONFIG_FB, no CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION), so no /dev/fb0 is
created.  Without enabling CONFIG_FB (or CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION on the
virtio-gpu/i915 DRM driver) the `doom` binary cannot display; use `doombench`
to verify the engine without a framebuffer.
